WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:04.360
As the sun's magnetic
field ripples out, it

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:05.860
twists and tangles.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.980
The lines criss cross
and reconnect, forming

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:12.930
giant magnetic bubbles.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:19.750
Each foamy sphere bubble
thing is about 100

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:21.940
million miles across,
which is tremendous.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:23.740
That's the distance from
the earth to the sun.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:26.020
And there are thousands
of these surrounding the

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:26.980
solar system.

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:31.990
No one is sure what these
magnetic bubbles do, but

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:35.650
it's possible that this
cosmic bubble wrap is the

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:37.690
first line of defense
against galactic

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:40.780
radiation, helping
protect the solar system

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:41.710
from the cosmic
onslaught.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.310
Think about how much
technology has changed

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:48.190
since Voyager was
launched, and today no

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:49.210
one had phones in their
pockets.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.310
I mean, no one could
even make long distance

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:53.260
phone calls across the
ocean without it being a

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:54.370
tremendous ordeal.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.530
But yet with this old
technology, we're now

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:58.630
learning about the very
edge of our solar system.

